00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:11.499
behind this specific workflow.

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:15.753
Now, jumping on to the tips, which is
the third part of my presentation.

00:03:15.973 --> 00:03:21.000
The first tip that I realized was extremely
necessary when testing my own server

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.760
on the cloud is to make sure you test
every gate possibly with cases that

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:30.720
fail. So send it on purpose on every run,
because you're currently changing your own

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.233
workflows. For example, via vibe coding.

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:35.207
Basically, how does this look in practice?

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.613
You're chatting with Claude and you say,
OK, I want to add this feature to the

00:03:39.653 --> 00:03:41.233
workflow. I want to add whatever.

00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:43.467
And Claude will change the workflows.

00:03:43.627 --> 00:03:47.940
And as a measure of safety, if on every
run you have this gate check where it

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:52.840
purposely sends a row that must fail and
one that must pass the gate, if there was

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.547
any alteration to the gate that makes it
either block both rows or pass both rows,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.213
it will block the system and email you
so that you know something is wrong.

00:04:01.253 --> 00:04:05.993
So you need to set up this gate in order
to ensure your whole system won't harm you

00:04:06.053 --> 00:04:09.466
in any way or do something that is
not up to your own expectations.

00:04:09.693 --> 00:04:13.099
I have a few quick examples that I'll
just skip through quite quickly.

00:04:13.279 --> 00:04:15.486
You can jump onto them later if you want.

00:04:15.686 --> 00:04:17.953
On the presentation deck,
there will be a QR code.

00:04:18.159 --> 00:04:22.500
So for example, I reordered my sheets
list and then suddenly the banner

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.387
row, which he considered to
be the first row, was empty.

00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:30.707
And therefore, he flagged
the execution as a success.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.020
And again, the common problem here is most
of the hardest problems to detect are not

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.640
the ones that specifically say there was
an error, are the ones that say success,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.573
right? So it has a success tag
and you assume everything is OK.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.600
But a lot of times the success
hides deeper problems that

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.507
via having your own n8n server,

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:54.187
as I mentioned earlier, you
can query the database, you can

00:04:54.287 --> 00:04:55.960
detect in many different ways.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.507
So this is one of the examples I
could tell you what I did to solve

00:04:59.647 --> 00:05:03.060
each, but I'm going to skip
those in order to speed up.

00:05:03.287 --> 00:05:07.353
I also had a problem with an if node that
was a formula that was only compatible

00:05:07.393 --> 00:05:11.743
with an older version of n8n and
therefore it assumed this formula was also

00:05:11.803 --> 00:05:15.850
compatible with the current n8n version,
which I was running and therefore it would

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.963
simply choose the wrong gates
and assume the rules were false.

00:05:19.203 --> 00:05:22.620
And proceed to the next step on
information that was simply wrong.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:27.767
And of course, the execution in the end
ran as normal and the step was classified

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.013
as successful. Now I have
also another example, the

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:35.533
output where there was a banner row that
was empty, basically to write the sheet as

00:05:35.613 --> 00:05:39.467
if there were no companies to separate,
and therefore the next step which was to

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:44.000
fetch the emails on the emails column was
automatically called off because of this,

00:05:44.213 --> 00:05:48.967
therefore it's again quite important to
make sure, read your successes and try to

00:05:49.127 --> 00:05:54.413
create rules that prevent a false
success message in the execution panel.

00:05:54.613 --> 00:05:58.867
So you can see later in more detail on the
slides how I solved each of these issues,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:00.807
but I'm just skimming through the tips.

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:05.787
Overall, this is a thing I talked about
earlier, so I made two canaries, one that

00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:10.147
must pass, one that must stop, which runs
on every run, and therefore when there's a

00:06:10.207 --> 00:06:14.207
small change to the way the
gates function, I'm always sure

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.220
that nothing that I'm not expecting
to happen will ever happen.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.187
For example, an email that is sent to a
recruiter that I have not ticked OK to be

00:06:23.247 --> 00:06:24.300
sent to a recruiter.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.780
So tip number two, your credentials are
part of the deploy and when you're hosting

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.660
your own n8n server, a lot of issues come
about and I'm just giving you quick tips

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.993
on which issues are and how I solve them.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.253
So when you re-import anything into your
n8n, all your credentials are lost so

00:06:40.313 --> 00:06:42.553
they're not encrypted
into the cloud itself.

00:06:42.673 --> 00:06:46.980
So one quick solution is to simply
put your credentials in your own file.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.133
Your OAuth app in Google
only works when it's sent to

00:06:51.193 --> 00:06:54.720
production. If you're not doing that,
it happened to me and I realized it

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.613
by working with this model after
one week it simply stopped working.

00:06:58.713 --> 00:07:00.587
So you start getting failed runs.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.207
So make sure you put your
consent screen to production.

00:07:04.413 --> 00:07:08.247
Then again, on the second version of
n8n, this is very specific, you have to

00:07:08.347 --> 00:07:11.433
actually save. Like saving is not enough,
you have to publish your changes or

00:07:11.573 --> 00:07:13.900
otherwise it can really lead to errors.

00:07:13.967 --> 00:07:17.333
You assume the latest version is already
running when it's actually the oldest

00:07:17.413 --> 00:07:22.413
version running. So always publish your
changes and last tip, make sure AI is

00:07:22.493 --> 00:07:27.133
judging but your model is strong enough
to handle most of the dirty work.

00:07:27.353 --> 00:07:30.867
So I'm giving you a few
examples of how I set this up.

00:07:31.107 --> 00:07:35.173
The way or these specific workflows
where this tip was most applicable was

00:07:35.273 --> 00:07:39.000
specifically for fetching different
emails and categorizing them.

00:07:39.233 --> 00:07:43.813
And then sorting those emails and applying
the sheets in which I track all of my

00:07:43.893 --> 00:07:47.740
applications. And so this is a small
tweak that I had to do in order

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.393
to make the system work well.

00:07:49.473 --> 00:07:54.213
So for example, if I was fetching 200
emails, I was getting an error due to

00:07:54.347 --> 00:07:58.200
exceeding the maximum amount of
emails fetchable from Google.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.227
So I had to cap it down to 60.

00:08:00.467 --> 00:08:02.733
I also noticed that some
IDs were being swapped.

00:08:02.793 --> 00:08:06.287
So emails were being confused, specific
emails that had very long IDs.

00:08:06.387 --> 00:08:10.527
So I basically added an echo for
each subject and also numbered them.

00:08:10.753 --> 00:08:14.373
This is very specific. So this is like my
own experience and probably something that

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:19.780
would happen with you if you happen to also
integrate a model that analyzes emails

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.620
and try to make it as
efficient as possible.

00:08:21.833 --> 00:08:26.720
Again, as I mentioned earlier, I set up
the system to avoid running AI on emails

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.400
that had already been sent before.

00:08:28.627 --> 00:08:32.620
And basically what it does is I have a
memory that stores all the emails that have

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.620
already been analyzed by the AI.

00:08:34.827 --> 00:08:39.480
And that ensures that only new emails that
don't match the emails in the memory are

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.880
analyzed. So this is a simple step that
I'm pretty sure most of you are already

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.400
applying to your own processes.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:49.140
And in the end, this is basically the
structure for when the AI actually acts.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.147
So there's the gates. These things are
contained in the sheets where there's a

00:08:53.293 --> 00:08:57.073
column. I could show them to you later if
you want to come to me and I'll show you

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.713
the sheets with which I interact,
have OK columns, final email,

00:09:00.773 --> 00:09:01.773
etc. The

00:09:01.907 --> 00:09:06.207
AI workflow that I set up checks
whether each row has an OK or

00:09:06.267 --> 00:09:10.587
does not have an OK. If it does have an
OK, it sends them to the AI guard to

00:09:10.687 --> 00:09:13.533
basically make sure that
everything is actually into place.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.613
If there's anything with a specific range,
a specific interval of confidence that is

00:09:18.793 --> 00:09:22.840
off, in very simple terms, it blocks
the sending of the email or otherwise if

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.313
there's a confidence above
70% it sends the email.

